---
/*
  Sprint 2C Interview shell — static reproduction of the immutable golden
  master's Interview page (reference/newsstand-original/source/Newsstand -
  Full Site.dc.html, the `isInterview` sc-if block, ~lines 323-397).

  Content classification (Sprint 2 content-shell rule — see
  DESIGN_DEVIATIONS.md and AGENTS.md):
  - CLASS 1, structural/interface copy (The Interview / p.12, "Tour dates /
    the résumé", "The rider", "Instruments", "Also", status labels) is
    production-final visual language.
  - CLASS 2, everything else below (headline, handwritten notes, the
    interview subject narrative, Q&A, pull quote, rider requirements,
    instruments list, CTA narrative) is source-faithful reference/demo copy
    carried over verbatim to preserve composition and density. None of it
    is verified production content — it will be replaced with real copy in
    a later content checkpoint. Where that copy is ultimately stored
    (inline, Content Collection, or otherwise) is not decided by this shell
    and is not implied here.

  Sprint 2I update: the "Tour dates / the résumé" timeline's three rows
  (job/date/status text) are NO LONGER class-2 demo content. Per the
  project owner's explicit Sprint 2I decision, they now show the project
  owner's real job history, following the exact same "real content, not
  demo" precedent DESIGN_DEVIATIONS.md entry 16 established for Letters'
  contact info — see DESIGN_DEVIATIONS.md, Sprint 2I entry, for the record.
  A new "View the full résumé →" link below the timeline points to the new
  `/resume/` page. This does not modify or invalidate entries 5/6 (the
  timeline's approved contrast exceptions): those are keyed to selectors/
  node types (`.interview-timeline-heading`,
  `.interview-timeline__status--muted`), not to specific text, so they
  still apply correctly to this real content.
*/
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/interview.css';

const title = 'Forty players, one tempo, and a deploy on Sunday | CABALLERO!';
const description =
  'The Interview / p.12 — forty small decisions that only work if everyone hits the same beat. Demo content pending final Sprint 2 review.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="interview-page container">
    <header>
      <p class="interview-kicker">The Interview / p.12</p>
      <h1 class="interview-headline">
        Forty players, one tempo, and a deploy on Sunday
      </h1>
      <p class="interview-hand interview-hand--note">
        recorded over two long blacks, twice interrupted by a rehearsal call
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="interview-portraits">
      <div class="interview-portrait" data-reveal>
        <img
          src="https://picsum.photos/seed/caballero-desk/900/1100"
          width="900"
          height="1100"
          alt="Placeholder stock photo standing in for a desk portrait of the interview subject — temporary demo image, not an authentic photograph"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="interview-portrait" data-reveal>
        <img
          src="https://picsum.photos/seed/caballero-stage/900/1100"
          width="900"
          height="1100"
          alt="Placeholder stock photo standing in for an on-stage portrait of the interview subject — temporary demo image, not an authentic photograph"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="interview-layout">
      <div class="interview-body">
        <p class="interview-meta">
          John Vincent Caballero &middot; Cebu, Philippines &middot; five years
          in &middot; answers lightly edited
        </p>

        <div class="interview-qa" data-reveal>
          <p>
            <strong class="interview-qa__marker">Q</strong>
            &nbsp;You direct a marching band and you ship software. Which is harder?
          </p>
          <p>
            The band, easily. You cannot hotfix a field show at halftime. But
            they teach each other — both are forty small decisions that only
            work if everyone hits the same beat, and both fall apart the moment
            you stop rehearsing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="interview-qa__marker">Q</strong>
            &nbsp;Everyone wants AI in their product. What do you tell them?
          </p>
          <p>
            That most of what they want is an if-statement with good manners.
            I&rsquo;ll happily build the model part — after we&rsquo;ve found
            the boring 80% that doesn&rsquo;t need one. Clients think
            that&rsquo;s me talking them out of work. It&rsquo;s the opposite:
            it&rsquo;s what makes the model part survive contact with reality.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="interview-qa__marker">Q</strong>
            &nbsp;Full-stack, mobile, automation — isn&rsquo;t that spread thin?
          </p>
          <p>
            It&rsquo;s one skill wearing three coats: I like owning the thing
            from the first conversation to the deploy. Small teams don&rsquo;t
            need a specialist for each layer, they need someone who won&rsquo;t
            hand off the awkward middle.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="interview-qa__marker">Q</strong>
            &nbsp;A bar-gig setlist saved a launch?
          </p>
          <p>
            Sort of. I built Setlist between two acoustic sets because I kept
            forgetting capo positions. The offline sync pattern I hacked
            together that night is exactly what shipped in the clinic app six
            months later.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="interview-qa__marker">Q</strong>
            &nbsp;Gacha games, manga, gadget reviews. Do those show up in the work?
          </p>
          <p>
            Constantly. Gacha UI is the most ruthlessly optimised interface
            design on earth — every tap is measured, every reward is paced.
            I&rsquo;ve stolen more from a pull animation than from most
            dashboards. And reviewing gadgets keeps me writing about things I
            use, which is the same muscle as writing a case study.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="interview-aside">
          <span class="interview-aside__mark" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
          <span class="interview-hand interview-hand--aside">
            he did in fact check his phone for the pull rates mid-answer. twice.
          </span>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="interview-quote" data-reveal>
          <p>
            &ldquo;Conducting is standup with a baton. Same tempo problem,
            better hats.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <cite>&mdash; J. V. Caballero</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <h2 class="interview-timeline-heading">Tour dates / the résumé</h2>
        <ol class="interview-timeline">
          <li class="interview-timeline__item">
            <div class="interview-timeline__row" data-reveal>
              <span class="interview-timeline__years">2025&mdash;Present</span>
              <div class="interview-timeline__body">
                <p class="interview-timeline__title">
                  Full-Stack Product Engineer &middot; Simple.biz
                </p>
                <p class="interview-timeline__desc">
                  Sole engineer delivering two production platforms end-to-end
                  &mdash; legal-services ops tooling and an EdTech coursework
                  system &mdash; plus AI-driven workflow automation.
                </p>
              </div>
              <span
                class="interview-timeline__status interview-timeline__status--current"
              >
                ON NOW
              </span>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="interview-timeline__item">
            <div class="interview-timeline__row" data-reveal>
              <span class="interview-timeline__years">2024&mdash;25</span>
              <div class="interview-timeline__body">
                <p class="interview-timeline__title">
                  Senior Developer &middot; TTEC Digital (Cisco)
                </p>
                <p class="interview-timeline__desc">
                  Modernized Cisco&rsquo;s internal Vue frontends and monitored
                  FedRAMP-grade production systems for U.S. federal clients.
                </p>
              </div>
              <span
                class="interview-timeline__status interview-timeline__status--muted"
              >
                SOLD OUT
              </span>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="interview-timeline__item">
            <div class="interview-timeline__row" data-reveal>
              <span class="interview-timeline__years">2021&mdash;24</span>
              <div class="interview-timeline__body">
                <p class="interview-timeline__title">
                  Full Stack Developer &middot; BPOSeats (now HQZen)
                </p>
                <p class="interview-timeline__desc">
                  Led a generative AI Kanban assistant and optimized Django/Vue
                  backend APIs at scale.
                </p>
              </div>
              <span
                class="interview-timeline__status interview-timeline__status--muted"
              >
                ARCHIVE
              </span>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <!--
          Sprint 2I: no `data-magnet-target` here on purpose.
          magnetic-navigation.ts uses a single `document.querySelector`
          (not `querySelectorAll`) scoped to '/interview/', so a second
          `[data-magnet-target]` node on this same route would silently
          steal or split that one-target behavior away from the existing
          Rotation CTA rather than cleanly extending to both. This link
          is deliberately a plain, understated bordered link instead of a
          second magnetic CTA — see DESIGN_DEVIATIONS.md.
        -->
        <a class="interview-resume-cta" href="/resume/">
          View the full résumé <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <aside class="interview-sidebar">
        <div class="interview-card" data-reveal>
          <h2 class="interview-card__heading">The rider</h2>
          <p class="interview-rider__intro">What I need to do good work:</p>
          <ul class="interview-rider__list">
            <li>&check; access to the people who&rsquo;ll actually use it</li>
            <li>&check; permission to delete things</li>
            <li>&check; one decision-maker, not a committee</li>
            <li>&check; two weeks before anyone asks for a demo</li>
            <li>&check; coffee, and a wall I can tape paper to</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="interview-card interview-card--instruments" data-reveal>
          <h2 class="interview-card__heading">Instruments</h2>
          <p class="interview-instruments">
            TypeScript &middot; React &middot; Next.js &middot; React Native
            &middot; Node &middot; Postgres &middot; n8n &middot; LLM
            orchestration &amp; evals &middot; Tailwind &middot; Docker &middot;
            a baton &middot; an acoustic guitar
          </p>
        </div>

        <a class="interview-cta" href="/rotation/" data-magnet-target>
          <span class="interview-cta__label">Also</span>
          <span class="interview-cta__title">
            What I&rsquo;m doing right now <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span
            >
          </span>
        </a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
